# Line endings — decided 2026-08-17, after a session of "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings.
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG, because it was not what the warnings suggested. `git ls-files --eol`
# reported `i/lf` for EVERY tracked text file: the repository content was already uniformly LF and
# had been all along, because Git for Windows sets `core.autocrlf=true` system-wide and normalises
# on commit. The mess was entirely in the WORKING TREE — 207 files LF, 126 CRLF and 11 mixed —
# because `autocrlf` converts to CRLF on checkout while the tools used to edit here write LF. So
# nothing needed repairing in history; what was missing was a statement of intent that both ends
# agree on.
#
# THE DECISION IS LF EVERYWHERE, in the repository and in the working tree, on every platform.
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf`, so this file settles it for every contributor without anyone
# having to configure anything, which is the point of putting it in the repository rather than in a
# machine's git config.
#
# Why LF rather than CRLF:
#   - CI runs on ubuntu-latest, and `eng/*.sh` are executed by bash there. A CRLF shell script
#     fails with the notoriously unhelpful `$'\r': command not found`.
#   - Every tool in this repo's loop — dotnet, the shell scripts, the editors — reads LF happily on
#     Windows. Visual Studio and VS Code both save LF without complaint when told to.
#   - It makes the working tree byte-identical to what is committed, so `git diff` never shows a
#     whole file as changed because someone's editor rewrote the endings.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts: stated explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`, because this is the one case
# where getting it wrong breaks CI rather than merely producing noise.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files genuinely want CRLF — cmd.exe can mis-parse an LF-only `.bat` in some
# constructs. There are none in the repository today; this is here so that adding one later does
# not require rediscovering why it misbehaves.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch the bytes, never try to diff them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary
*.db binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary

# Better hunk headers in diffs. Cosmetic, and cheap.
*.cs   diff=csharp
*.md   diff=markdown
*.json diff=json
